six('lem-yext-exact-sequences-in-both-variables','-The fixed short exact sequence is not an empty sequence of variable length.|Proof step 1.2 and Proof step 2.3 use universal pullbacks/pushouts and work when any permitted object is zero.|-There is no numerical field-size or one-dimensional hypothesis in this general abelian-category exactness result.|Proof step 3.1 identifies the last kernel through zero Yoneda splice; it does not assume Ext2=0 or assert a final surjection.|-The last displayed Ext2 term is a sequence endpoint, not an interval boundary; no exactness beyond the displayed terms is asserted.|The lifts in Proof step 1.2 and Proof step 2.3 are individual witnesses supplied by universal constructions, not a family chosen over all extensions.')
six('def-finite-field-hereditary-hall-category','Definition: an abelian category has a zero object, so an empty category cannot be an example.|Definition: Hom(0,X) and Ext1(0,X) are zero vector spaces of cardinality one; zero objects are allowed.|Definition: the finite-field vector-space example has a one-dimensional simple object and Ext2=0; rank-one objects are not excluded.|Definition: Ext2=0 is required; categories with nonzero Ext2 are outside the hereditary domain, not cases silently covered.|-There is no interval or chart parameter; exact-sequence endpoints are categorical objects.|Definition: the small category and finite-dimensional Hom/Ext spaces are supplied. The property does not choose representatives of every isomorphism class.')
six('lem-finite-loopless-quiver-nilpotent-category-is-hereditary-finitary','With no vertices the only representation is zero and all Hom/Ext spaces are zero; the stated hereditary and finitary properties hold.|Proof step 1.1 includes zero representations and the explicit extension construction in Proof step 3.1 includes zero blocks.|For one loopless vertex there are no arrows, so representations are finite vector spaces and Ext1=Ext2=0.|Proof step 1.1 uses the r+s path bound even for cyclic quivers; no acyclicity is silently added to the nilpotent domain.|-There is no interval or numerical endpoint parameter in the categorical finiteness assertion.|Proof step 2.1 uses the fixed finite-field ordering for a skeleton; Proof step 3.1 only chooses finite-dimensional splitting/arrow coordinates for the given extension.')
six('def-euler-twisted-hall-algebra','Definition: a finite-support element may have empty support, in which case it is the zero vector and multiplies to zero.|Definition: the zero-object basis element [0] is the unit, whereas the zero linear combination is the additive zero.|Definition: scalar one is 1·[0]; a one-vertex category uses the same ν-Euler formula.|Definition: q≥2 and ν>1; Euler value zero gives twist factor one without excluding such pairs.|-There is no numerical interval parameter; the ordering of extension endpoints fixes the product convention.|Definition: each input is a finite linear combination and every coefficient is a fixed finite count, so no unspecified selection is needed.')
six('def-green-hall-completion-and-coproduct','Definition: an empty coefficient family contributes zero; the completion is a product of scalar coefficient slots, not an unrestricted convolution algebra.|Definition: a0=1 and χ(0,0)=0 give Δ([0])=[0]⊗[0] and counit([0])=1.|Definition: the scalar unit in degree (0,0) remains one, with zero-object automorphism order one.|Definition: all automorphism denominators are positive integers; infinite total coproduct support is permitted coefficientwise.|-The index slots are isomorphism-class pairs, not an interval with endpoints.|Definition: the coefficient formula uses finite counts at a fixed pair; no simultaneous choice of extension representatives is required.')
six('def-extended-hall-algebra-and-composition-subalgebra','Definition: an empty set of simple generators still generates a unital coefficient-field algebra, and K0=0 gives the trivial torus.|Definition: K0[0] is the multiplicative unit and is distinct from the zero vector.|Definition: one loopless vertex retains the torus conjugation factor ν² and its simple generator.|Definition: Euler pairings may vanish, giving conjugation factor one; q=1 is excluded by the finite-field parameter.|-The torus index is an abelian group, not a bounded interval.|Definition: torus symbols, finite linear combinations and the generated subalgebra are specified algebraically; no arbitrary representatives are selected.')
six('thm-prime-power-specializations-of-the-integral-quantum-half-have-a-nondegenerate-green-form','The empty vertex set has only the vacuum and its Gram matrix is (1), so specialization is nondegenerate.|The degree-zero vacuum has norm one; only the zero vector has zero positive Hall norm.|The rank-one Gram factors are nonzero quantum factorials at ν=√q>1.|The constant-term argument excludes zeros at genuine prime-power ν; it does not assert nondegeneracy at roots of unity or at q=1.|-The ν-domain is the set of specified prime-power square roots, not an interval-endpoint statement.|The integral homogeneous basis and bar data are supplied by the cited integral-form results; each determinant is finite, with no new arbitrary-index choice.')
six('lem-positive-green-pairing-specializes-the-integral-half-into-the-composition-algebra','With no vertices the lattice and its Hall image are the coefficient field and the map sends the vacuum to [0].|The zero vector has norm zero, while the degree-zero vacuum maps to the nonzero unit [0].|At one vertex the divided powers map to the specified simple-object powers; their quantum-factorial coefficients are nonzero at ν>1.|Positivity is used over the stated real ν=√q and does not claim an analogous argument for roots of unity.|-There is no bounded interval or chart endpoint in the specialization map.|The chosen integral basis is inherited input; every relation and norm computation involves only finitely many homogeneous coefficients.')
six('lem-finite-dynkin-quiver-reflection-classification','For the empty ADE forest there are no nonzero indecomposables or positive roots, so the classification is empty on both sides.|The zero representation is not indecomposable; reflection permits zero kernels away from the excluded sink/source simple summand.|For A1 the unique indecomposable is the one-dimensional simple, with endomorphism field k and no self-extension.|The inverse reflection assertion is restricted off the sink/source simple summand; no inverse is claimed there without the displayed restriction.|-The finite reflection order is not an interval parameter with a limiting endpoint.|Finite root/reflection orders and bases are the classical linear-algebra data used in the construction; no additional arbitrary-index choice is introduced.')
six('lem-finite-dynkin-hall-algebra-is-simple-generated','Proof step 3.1 treats the empty quiver as the coefficient field with unit and no simple generators.|The unit [0] is generated as an empty product and is not the zero vector.|For A1, powers of the simple class generate all dimensions since the flag coefficients are nonzero.|Finite ADE is required; the triangular root ordering is not claimed for affine or indefinite quivers.|-The finite root order is a proof ordering, not a numerical interval boundary.|The proof uses one finite root order and finite flag counts inherited from reflection classification.')
six('lem-finite-field-schur-descent-and-restriction','The Schur configurations used for the counting statement are nonempty for sufficiently large extension degree because the explicit lower bound is positive.|A Schur object is nonzero; the zero object has zero endomorphism algebra and is excluded by End=field.|Descent works also for extension degree one; only the asymptotic counting estimate requires a sufficiently large degree.|The norm argument uses a genuine finite-field extension and its cyclic Galois group; it is not an arbitrary-field descent claim.|-The sufficiently-large integer threshold is not a claimed asymptotic endpoint value at a real limit.|Algebraic closures and standard finite-field linear algebra are inherited foundations; for a fixed object the norm preimage and basis choices are finite-dimensional.')
six('lem-affine-de-quivers-have-exponentially-many-indecomposables','The constructed Schur configuration locus contains an explicit point and its big-cell open subset supplies positive counts for all sufficiently large extensions.|The counted objects have the fixed nonzero dimension vector; the zero representation is not an indecomposable witness.|The result concerns the specified affine D/E diagrams; one-vertex graphs are outside these hypotheses.|The configuration and descent argument covers the stated finite fields and orientations; no small-characteristic restriction is silently added.|-The integer extension-degree threshold is explicit eventual behavior, not an interval endpoint assertion.|The proof uses explicit configurations, finitely many coordinates and the supplied finite-field descent, with inherited algebraic-geometry foundations.')
six('lem-hall-composition-full-exactly-finite-dynkin','The empty quiver is explicitly included: its Hall and composition algebras are both the coefficient field.|Degree zero on either side is the unit line spanned by [0], not the zero algebra.|A single loopless vertex is A1, where the Hall algebra is simple-generated.|Non-Dynkin parallel-arrow, cycle and affine D/E configurations provide the obstruction; the proof retains all cases rather than assuming a tree.|-There is no interval parameter in the graph-theoretic equivalence.|Finite subquiver choices and the stated classification suppliers provide the witnesses; the classical foundations of those suppliers are inherited.')
six('lem-numerical-serre-rank-one-skew-kernel-is-integrable','-A fixed vertex i is part of the hypotheses, so the vertex set cannot be empty.|The degree-zero vacuum belongs to the skew kernel and has the trivial finite i-string; the final nonzero-vector bound excludes z=0.|For a one-vertex graph the remaining skew-kernel generators are absent and only the constant highest vector remains.|Quantum integers at ν>1 are nonzero; the proof does not assert this root-string argument at roots of unity.|-Root-string indices are discrete algebraic degrees and no limiting interval endpoint is claimed.|All skew-derivative and word calculations involve finite words and finite sums; no additional arbitrary-index selection is needed.')
six('lem-the-nondegenerate-integral-specialization-has-a-quantum-casimir','An empty root set leaves the quasi-R series with its sole empty term 1 and the Casimir acts on the vacuum.|The vacuum weight is zero and its scalar factor is one; a zero input vector is sent to zero.|The rank-one construction has the usual finite acting string on each bounded-weight vector.|The local finiteness hypothesis is essential: only finitely many quasi-R terms act on each vector; no convergence of an arbitrary infinite vector sum is asserted.|-The finite weight support condition is not a numerical interval-endpoint claim.|The PBW dual bases are inherited from the nondegenerate integral form; all evaluations on a fixed vector are finite.')
six('lem-a-minimal-numerical-serre-kernel-obeys-the-quantum-casimir-constraint','-The hypotheses assume a nonzero residual kernel, impossible for an empty generator set.|The chosen kernel weight has strictly positive height, since degree zero maps identically; a zero weight is excluded.|-There is no one-dimensional boundary assumption; the lemma is conditional on a nonzero minimal homogeneous kernel.|The contradiction uses ν>1 and the supplied Casimir; roots of unity and zero kernel do not satisfy the assumed contradiction setup.|-Minimal positive integer height is not an interval endpoint or analytic limit.|Well-ordering supplies the least positive height and then one nonzero witness at that height; this is not a simultaneous family choice.')
